                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1508-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1508-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1508-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Samson CL8</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Samson CL8 is a large-diaphragm multipattern studio condenser. It is the multipattern version of the company&#8217;s CL7, which shares a body shape, capsule design, and most other features.</p><p>The capsule is described as a 1.1-inch model, which we interpret to mean 32mm O.D. with 25mm diaphragms. It is center-terminated, and likely a K67 variant as is common to Asian-manufactured microphones. The diaphragm material is described as 3-micron on the Samson website, or 4-micron in the PDF manual.</p><p>Two switches on the front of the mic&#8217;s body enable the pad (-10dB) and high-pass filter (-12dB/octave at 100Hz). A switch on the rear allows selection of Cardioid, Omni, or Figure-8 patterns.</p><p>The mic ships with hard mount and a foam-lined plastic carrying case. An elastic shockmount, model SP01, is available separately.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Samson/CL8">Samson CL8</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2004.</p>
